1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,173
This video solves the hardest automata design question in the question bank. Accept the binary numbers that are divisible by 5.

2
00:00:09,623 --> 00:00:21,015
We find what the machine must remember, build the D F A, test it, and minimize it. Then we look at the regular expression, and we check the posted answers in python.

3
00:00:21,465 --> 00:00:25,263
Here is the question. The input is a binary integer.

4
00:00:25,713 --> 00:00:28,337
The most significant bit is on the left.

5
00:00:28,787 --> 00:00:33,117
Leading zeros are allowed, so 0 0 1 0 is 2.

6
00:00:33,567 --> 00:00:36,554
Accept the numbers that are divisible by 5.

7
00:00:37,004 --> 00:00:44,812
Above each bit is its place value. 1 0 1 is 4 plus 1, which is 5. Divisible by 5.

8
00:00:45,262 --> 00:00:49,870
1 0 1 0 is 8 plus 2, which is 10. Divisible.

9
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:56,229
1 1 1 1 is 8 plus 4 plus 2 plus 1, which is 15. Divisible.

10
00:00:56,679 --> 00:01:00,775
0 is 0. 0 is also divisible by 5.

11
00:01:01,225 --> 00:01:03,935
1 0 is 2. Not divisible.

12
00:01:04,385 --> 00:01:09,419
1 1 1 is 4 plus 2 plus 1, which is 7. Not divisible.

13
00:01:09,869 --> 00:01:15,608
0 0 1 0 is 2. The leading zeros add nothing. Not divisible.

14
00:01:16,058 --> 00:01:21,029
1 1 0 0 is 8 plus 4, which is 12. Not divisible.

15
00:01:21,479 --> 00:01:32,764
The posted solution says the question is too hard as a regex. It should ask for a D F A. So we build the D F A first. That is the method to remember.

16
00:01:33,214 --> 00:01:42,323
A D F A reads the bits from left to right, one bit at a time. So first ask: what happens to the value when one more bit arrives?

17
00:01:42,773 --> 00:01:45,589
Before any bit is read, the value is 0.

18
00:01:46,039 --> 00:01:49,282
Read 1. 2 times 0, plus 1, is 1.

19
00:01:49,732 --> 00:01:59,119
Read 1. The old bits move one place to the left, so the old value doubles. Then add the new bit. 2 times 1, plus 1, is 3.

20
00:01:59,569 --> 00:02:03,110
Read 0. 2 times 3, plus 0, is 6.

21
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:06,888
Read 1. 2 times 6, plus 1, is 13.

22
00:02:07,338 --> 00:02:11,861
So the rule is: new value equals 2 times old value, plus the bit.

23
00:02:12,311 --> 00:02:19,628
But the value grows without limit. A D F A has a finite number of states, so it cannot store the value.

24
00:02:20,078 --> 00:02:25,134
We do not need the value. We only need to know if it is divisible by 5.

25
00:02:25,584 --> 00:02:31,344
Here are the values for the input 1 1 0 1: 1, 3, 6 and 13.

26
00:02:31,794 --> 00:02:35,463
Draw each value as dots, in full rows of 5.

27
00:02:35,913 --> 00:02:41,204
The dots that are left over, in orange, give the remainder when you divide by 5.

28
00:02:41,654 --> 00:02:44,662
So the remainders are 1, 3, 1 and 3.

29
00:02:45,112 --> 00:02:51,448
Now read one more 1. The new value is 2 times 13, plus 1, which is 27.

30
00:02:51,898 --> 00:02:58,682
27 is five full rows of 5, and 2 left over. So 27 mod 5 is 2.

31
00:02:59,132 --> 00:03:03,335
Can we get this 2 without 13? Take the old remainder, 3.

32
00:03:03,785 --> 00:03:11,785
2 times 3, plus 1, is 7. And 7 mod 5 is 2. That is the same remainder as for 27.

33
00:03:12,235 --> 00:03:22,560
The reason: write v as 5 q plus r, where r is the remainder. Then 2 v plus b is 5 times 2 q, plus 2 r plus b.

34
00:03:23,010 --> 00:03:30,093
5 times 2 q is a multiple of 5. So only 2 r plus b decides the next remainder.

35
00:03:30,543 --> 00:03:35,300
A python check confirmed this rule for every value up to 2000.

36
00:03:35,750 --> 00:03:40,401
So the states are the five remainders: 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4.

37
00:03:40,851 --> 00:03:45,608
Before any bit is read, the value is 0. So the start state is 0.

38
00:03:46,058 --> 00:03:53,738
A number is divisible by 5 when its remainder is 0. So state 0 is the only accepting state.

39
00:03:54,188 --> 00:04:03,212
Now fill the table with the rule. On a 0, the next state is 2 s mod 5. On a 1, it is 2 s plus 1, mod 5.

40
00:04:03,662 --> 00:04:10,446
From state 0: 2 times 0 is 0. On 0 we go to 0. On 1 we go to 1.

41
00:04:10,896 --> 00:04:16,891
From state 1: 2 times 1 is 2. On 0 we go to 2. On 1 we go to 3.

42
00:04:17,341 --> 00:04:25,767
From state 2: 2 times 2 is 4. On 0 we go to 4. On 1 we get 5, and 5 mod 5 is 0.

43
00:04:26,217 --> 00:04:34,388
From state 3: 2 times 3 is 6. On 0, 6 mod 5 is 1. On 1, 7 mod 5 is 2.

44
00:04:34,838 --> 00:04:42,881
From state 4: 2 times 4 is 8. On 0, 8 mod 5 is 3. On 1, 9 mod 5 is 4.

45
00:04:43,331 --> 00:04:46,787
Every state now has one next state for each bit.

46
00:04:47,237 --> 00:04:54,874
Now draw the D F A from the table. One circle per state. State 0 is the start state, and it accepts.

47
00:04:55,324 --> 00:05:00,444
From state 0: on 0 it loops on 0. On 1 it goes to 1.

48
00:05:00,894 --> 00:05:04,393
From 1: 0 goes to 2, and 1 goes to 3.

49
00:05:04,843 --> 00:05:08,811
From 2: 0 goes to 4, and 1 goes back to 0.

50
00:05:09,261 --> 00:05:12,738
From 3: 0 goes to 1, and 1 goes to 2.

51
00:05:13,188 --> 00:05:16,900
From 4: 0 goes to 3, and 1 loops on 4.

52
00:05:17,350 --> 00:05:26,779
The machine is total: every state has a 0 arrow and a 1 arrow. No error state is needed, because every bit string is a number.

53
00:05:27,229 --> 00:05:34,611
The posted solution draws the same machine. It uses solid lines for 1 and dotted lines for 0.

54
00:05:35,061 --> 00:05:41,269
Run the machine. After each prefix, the state is the value of that prefix, mod 5.

55
00:05:41,719 --> 00:05:47,244
Input 1 0 1 0. The state goes 0, 1, 2, 0, 0.

56
00:05:47,694 --> 00:05:55,587
The prefixes 1, 1 0, 1 0 1 and 1 0 1 0 have the values 1, 2, 5 and 10.

57
00:05:56,037 --> 00:06:01,243
Their remainders mod 5 are 1, 2, 0 and 0. These are the states.

58
00:06:01,693 --> 00:06:06,791
The run ends in state 0, so accept. 10 is divisible by 5.

59
00:06:07,241 --> 00:06:11,337
Input 1 1 1. The state goes 0, 1, 3, 2.

60
00:06:11,787 --> 00:06:18,187
The value is 7, and 7 mod 5 is 2. The run ends in state 2, so reject.

61
00:06:18,637 --> 00:06:27,277
Input 0 0 1 0, with leading zeros. State 0 loops on 0, so the leading zeros change nothing.

62
00:06:27,727 --> 00:06:32,911
Then 1 goes to 1, and 0 goes to 2. The value is 2, so reject.

63
00:06:33,361 --> 00:06:43,196
Check the machine. Each box shows a number, its binary form, and the state where the run ends. 0 to 4 end in states 0 to 4.

64
00:06:43,646 --> 00:06:46,931
5 to 9 end in states 0 to 4 again.

65
00:06:47,381 --> 00:06:54,379
10 to 14, and 15 to 19, repeat the pattern. The state is always the number mod 5.

66
00:06:54,829 --> 00:07:01,271
The multiples of 5 end in state 0, so they are accepted: 0, 5, 10 and 15.

67
00:07:01,721 --> 00:07:13,519
A python check ran the D F A on every bit string up to length 14, about 32 thousand strings. It agrees with the rule: the value mod 5 is 0.

68
00:07:13,969 --> 00:07:24,977
The empty string also ends in state 0, as value 0. The question says nothing about it. To reject it, add a new start state that is not accepting.

69
00:07:25,427 --> 00:07:33,576
Is five states the smallest? Run the minimization. Here is the table. The machine is total, so there is no error state.

70
00:07:34,026 --> 00:07:39,359
Begin with two bubbles: state 0 accepts, and 1, 2, 3, 4 do not.

71
00:07:39,809 --> 00:07:43,137
For each state, write the bubble that each bit leads to.

72
00:07:43,587 --> 00:07:48,857
Round 1. On 1, state 2 goes to state 0, in the accepting bubble.

73
00:07:49,307 --> 00:07:55,323
On 1, states 1, 3 and 4 stay in bubble 1 2 3 4. So 2 splits off.

74
00:07:55,773 --> 00:08:01,789
Round 2. Write the rows again with three bubbles: 0, then 1 3 4, then 2.

75
00:08:02,239 --> 00:08:06,868
On 0, state 1 goes to state 2, which is now its own bubble.

76
00:08:07,318 --> 00:08:11,755
On 1, state 3 goes to state 2. That is bubble 2 again.

77
00:08:12,205 --> 00:08:18,925
State 4 stays in bubble 1 3 4 on both bits. So the bubble 1 3 4 splits into three.

78
00:08:19,375 --> 00:08:23,322
Now every bubble has one state, so the machine is minimal.

79
00:08:23,772 --> 00:08:35,527
For example, the string 0 1 separates 1 and 3. From 1 it goes to 2, then to 0, and accepts. From 3 it goes to 1, then back to 3, and rejects.

80
00:08:35,977 --> 00:08:38,494
The technique works for any divisor n.

81
00:08:38,944 --> 00:08:43,637
Use n states: one for each remainder, 0 to n minus 1.

82
00:08:44,087 --> 00:08:46,349
Start and accept in state 0.

83
00:08:46,799 --> 00:08:50,383
On bit b, go from s to 2 s plus b, mod n.

84
00:08:50,833 --> 00:08:56,849
For n equals 3, you get three states. A python check confirms it up to length 12.

85
00:08:57,299 --> 00:09:01,331
For n equals 4, the remainder machine has four states.

86
00:09:01,781 --> 00:09:10,762
States 1 and 3 have equal rows: on 0 both go to 2, and on 1 both go to 3. Neither accepts. So they merge.

87
00:09:11,212 --> 00:09:13,559
The minimal machine has three states.

88
00:09:14,009 --> 00:09:20,451
It accepts the strings that end in 0 0, and also the strings 0 and the empty string.

89
00:09:20,901 --> 00:09:27,536
So the remainder machine is always correct, but it is not always minimal. For 5, it is minimal.

90
00:09:27,986 --> 00:09:33,639
This part is optional. Converting a D F A into a regex is not on the slides.

91
00:09:34,089 --> 00:09:37,183
The posted solution gives a short regex.

92
00:09:37,633 --> 00:09:50,838
Test it with 1 1 0 0 1. That is 16 plus 8 plus 1, which is 25. The D F A goes 0, 1, 3, 1, 2, 0, so it accepts 25.

93
00:09:51,288 --> 00:10:00,013
But our python check shows that the short regex rejects 1 1 0 0 1. So the short regex is not correct.

94
00:10:00,463 --> 00:10:12,111
The posted long derivation also has errors. One of its parts is 1, then 1 0 star. It matches the string 1, and 1 is not divisible by 5.

95
00:10:12,561 --> 00:10:17,575
Here is a regex that passes the check on every string up to length 16.

96
00:10:18,025 --> 00:10:24,019
Read it as paths that start and end in state 0. The outer star repeats such paths.

97
00:10:24,469 --> 00:10:27,477
One path is the 0 loop on state 0.

98
00:10:27,927 --> 00:10:31,213
The other path starts with 1, from 0 to 1.

99
00:10:31,663 --> 00:10:35,311
1 0 star loops from 1 to 3 and back to 1.

100
00:10:35,761 --> 00:10:38,641
0, or 1 1, goes from 1 to 2.

101
00:10:39,091 --> 00:10:47,005
In the middle, 0 1 star 0 1 goes from 2 to 4, loops on 4, then goes to 3, and back to 2.

102
00:10:47,455 --> 00:10:58,399
The second kind, 0 1 star 0 0, goes from 2 to 4 and 3, then to 1. Then 1 0 star, and 0 or 1 1, return to 2.

103
00:10:58,849 --> 00:11:01,473
The last 1 goes from 2 back to 0.

104
00:11:01,923 --> 00:11:06,382
On an exam, give the D F A first. It is short and easy to check.

105
00:11:06,832 --> 00:11:12,976
To summarize. This video built a D F A for the binary numbers divisible by 5.

106
00:11:13,426 --> 00:11:17,607
When a bit b arrives, the value v becomes 2 v plus b.

107
00:11:18,057 --> 00:11:23,860
Only the remainder mod 5 matters, and the remainder alone gives the next remainder.

108
00:11:24,310 --> 00:11:36,321
So use five states, one per remainder. Start and accept in 0. On bit b, go from s to 2 s plus b, mod 5. Leading zeros loop on state 0.

109
00:11:36,771 --> 00:11:42,403
Test the machine with numbers, and minimize it. For 5, all five states are needed.

110
00:11:42,853 --> 00:11:51,023
On an exam, build the D F A first. The posted short regex fails our check, for example on 25.
